[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. The reports in it say
otherwise. They share a symptom (the date is wrong), not a cause, so one fix will not close them all.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix leaves two problems open. Each now has its own ticket.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

- **parser**: the shared parser reads every date month first, and every tenant on file writes the
  day first. `7/4` (7 April) becomes 4 July.
- **hint**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People follow it and type
  month first, and the form reads the date day first. The parser fix does not reach these.
- **shift**: the date moves by one day, e.g. "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got
  Monday." No day and month swap here. The desk's own notes list these as a one-day move.

## How they were split

Same rules the kb already runs (`#lib/kb/causes`): the cause comes from the tenant's locale, its
booking form and the channel, not from the subject line or the intake tag. On top of that, the
reports in `desk-notes.json` under `oneDayMove` count as their own problem.

## Why one ticket hid three

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports on subject alone. A subject
line says the date is wrong; it cannot say why. The decision is accepted, so it stays as written;
a new record should replace it.

